  "title": "CHAPTER 9 : ELECTRICITY",
  "order": 102,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "2je3w0r",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO ELECTRICITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric charge is the fundamental property responsible for electric current and potential difference.",
        "Ohm's Law governs the linear relationship between voltage and electric current across a conductor.",
        "Resistance determines the opposition to current flow and depends on material resistivity and geometry.",
        "Electric power and electrical energy represent the rate and total consumption of electrical work.",
        "The heating effect of electric current powers thermal appliances and enables safety devices like electric fuses."
      ],
      "id": "gq04g6s"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Chapter Number",
          "value": "Chapter 9",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Law",
          "value": "Ohm's Law",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Phenomenon",
          "value": "Heating Effect",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Safety Device",
          "value": "Electric Fuse",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "pxdoc0z"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8otk52c",
      "content": "Electricity is a form of energy produced due to the flow of electric charges. In daily life, electricity is used in:"
    },
    {
      "id": "c22vlp1",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Lighting</li><li>Fans</li><li>Heaters</li><li>Mobile phones</li><li>Computers</li></ul>",
        "Lighting",
        "Fans",
        "Heaters",
        "Mobile phones",
        "Computers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ldwxugm",
      "content": "Electricity plays a very important role in modern life."
    },
    {
      "id": "uqk054h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC CHARGE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dw2i6vp",
      "content": "Electric charge is the basic property of matter responsible for electrical phenomena. Types of charges:"
    },
    {
      "id": "u93o9ay",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Positive charge<img></img></li><li>Negative charge</li></ul>",
        "Positive charge<img></img>",
        "Negative charge"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u1jb6r9",
      "content": "Like charges repel each other. Unlike charges attract each other."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rjhh179",
      "content": "SI unit of charge = Coulomb (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ocuv9ut",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2k68moc",
      "content": "Electric current is the rate of flow of electric charge. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "su8s4b6",
      "content": "Current (I) = Charge (Q) / Time (t) SI unit of current = Ampere (A)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "094dwoj",
      "content": "1 Ampere means 1 Coulomb of charge flowing per second."
    },
    {
      "id": "ro3sfwf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC CIRCUIT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zftzrkv",
      "content": "An electric circuit is a closed path through which electric current flows. Main components:"
    },
    {
      "id": "k4ugvec",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Cell or battery</li><li>Connecting wires</li><li>Switch</li><li>Load (bulb, fan, etc.)</li></ul>",
        "Cell or battery",
        "Connecting wires",
        "Switch",
        "Load (bulb, fan, etc.)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "71ukwr3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4z0xlzu",
      "content": "Potential difference is the work done in moving a unit charge from one point to another. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l4vj3kw",
      "content": "V = Work / Charge SI unit = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "3ppue9o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "OHM’S LAW"
    },
    {
      "id": "uspwlit",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "vhnux26",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_vhnux26.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zqnyg03",
      "content": "According to Ohm’s Law:<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "CHAPTER 9 : ELECTRICITY",
      "id": "b7oxsfr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "98scdh4",
      "content": "The current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cgntloa",
      "content": "Formula: "
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "x7rd2g1",
      "content": "V = IR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nzxu2ha",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1g27rct",
      "content": "V = Voltage I = Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "quv3pry",
      "content": "R = Resistance"
    },
    {
      "id": "70j28ev",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "RESISTANCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ztzwiix",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered to the flow of electric current. SI unit = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "omow6dn",
      "content": "Factors affecting resistance:"
    },
    {
      "id": "4md6vcp",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Length of conductor</li><li>Area of cross-section</li><li>Material of conductor</li><li>Temperature</li></ul>",
        "Length of conductor",
        "Area of cross-section",
        "Material of conductor",
        "Temperature"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0z6fbto",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC POWER"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "knbvhk4",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Electric Current",
        "Electric Potential Difference"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Definition",
          "Rate of flow of electric charges",
          "Work done in moving a unit charge between two points"
        ],
        [
          "SI Unit",
          "Ampere (A)",
          "Volt (V)"
        ],
        [
          "Measuring Instrument",
          "Ammeter (connected in series)",
          "Voltmeter (connected in parallel)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "1o3kvzk"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jl61se5",
      "content": "Power (P) = V × I SI unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i5p2g1s",
      "content": "Other forms:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yxp0li9",
      "content": "P = I²R"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "q984m6y",
      "content": "P = V² / R"
    },
    {
      "id": "poft2ic",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6eu71sp",
      "content": "Electric energy is the total electrical work done. Commercial unit of electric energy = kilowatt-hour (kWh) 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aiziqmg",
      "content": "This is also called one unit of electricity."
    },
    {
      "id": "eprjzha",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "HEATING EFFECT OF ELECTRIC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uj79j3z",
      "content": "When electric current flows through a conductor, it produces heat. This effect is used in:"
    },
    {
      "id": "54kdpao",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Electric heater</li><li>Iron</li><li>Geyser</li><li>Electric bulb</li></ul>",
        "Electric heater",
        "Iron",
        "Geyser",
        "Electric bulb"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a0row3z",
      "content": "Heating effect depends on:"
    },
    {
      "id": "rnohqtr",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Current</li><li>Resistance</li><li>Time</li></ul>",
        "Current",
        "Resistance",
        "Time"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dqz3w99",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC FUSE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ppq3knq",
      "content": "Fuse is a safety device used to protect electrical appliances. It melts when excess current flows and breaks the circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Ohm's law verification and interpretation of V-I graphs are frequently tested.",
        "Distinction between resistance and resistivity, including combination of resistors (series vs. parallel), is critical for numericals.",
        "Working mechanism and rating calculations for an electric fuse based on the heating effect of current."
      ],
      "id": "yvy7lso"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d4rg5pf",
      "content": "Fuse wire is made of:"
    },
    {
      "id": "iu7814k",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Tin</li><li>Lead</li></ul>",
        "Tin",
        "Lead"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4x36un1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "IMPORTANT  EXAM QUESTIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ygtp7oe",
      "content": "Q1. SI unit of current? Ans: Ampere"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lhjvs9n",
      "content": "Q2. Formula of Ohm’s Law? Ans: V = IR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e4zw80r",
      "content": "Q3. Commercial unit of electricity? Ans: kWh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nzw5ufm",
      "content": "Q4. SI unit of resistance? Ans: Ohm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d2hm1il",
      "content": "Q5. Heating effect depends on? Ans: Current, resistance and time"
    },
    {
      "id": "r6m0i9c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "QUICK REVISION"
    },
    {
      "id": "cb2yto0",
